          "derivation": "Every form but one is exactly right. Av./OP grab- 'to seize' is Bartholomae's verb 'grab- V. \"greifen\"' (Altiranisches Wörterbuch col. 526, cited as such by Nyberg s.v. griftan) and Cheung's *grabH 'to grab, seize, take' (Etymological Dictionary of the Iranian Verb pp. 119-121). 'gerew-âiti, gerewn-aîti' = the nasal present YAv. gərəβnaiti (Yt 10.13, Y 57.25 garəβnąn); 'gâuru-ayeiti, garb-âyaiti' = the aya-present YAv. gouruuaiieiti / hangouruuaiieiti (Y 10.2) beside OP garba-ya- (imperfect agarbāyam DB 2.4, agarbāya DB 2.88, passive agṛbiya DB 2.73), Kent's Lexicon: 'OP garba-ya- in imf. agarbāyam ... to pAr. *gṛbha-, cf. Skt. gṛbhāyati' — the two stems Pokorny 455 lists side by side. 'ni-gerepto' is YAv. ni-gərəpta-, the ta-participle 'seized, held down', which Gharib quotes as the etymon of Sogdian nayruβ/fs- (Sogdian Dictionary p. 237, entry 5918 'nyrwβs- ... ©Av. nigərəpta ©med. pass. st.'); Nourai's gloss 'to be pressed down' is the Pahlavi-influenced reading of that passive, acceptable if loose. The weak item is 'paiti-grab': Cheung's inventory of Avestan preverb compounds of grabH lists *a-, *uz-, *fra- and *ham- only, not *pati-, and Bartholomae's article does not have paiti.grab-; the compound is securely Old Iranian all the same, since it is continued by MMP pdyr- / BMP padīr- 'accept, receive', Parthian pdgyrw-, Sogdian ptyrβ-/pcyrβ- 'to accept, receive, understand' — so *pati-grab- belongs on the chart, but under a reconstructed Old Iranian label rather than as an attested Avestan word.",
