          "derivation": "The Germanic verbal base *grab-/*grap- 'to snatch, seize' — Swedish grabba, Middle Low German and Middle Dutch grabben, grabbelen 'to catch, snatch', MHG grappeln 'to grope', Norwegian dial. grafsa/grapsa, German grapsen — is assigned to this root by Pokorny (p. 455, listing exactly these forms), by Klein ('MDu. grabben, rel. to OS. garva, MDu. garve, OHG. garba \"sheaf\", lit. \"that which is gathered up or together\", fr. I.-E. base *gherebh-, *ghrebh-, \"to seize\"') and by Watkins/AHD, the reference Nourai cites here. The formation is the zero/e-grade *gʰrbʰ-/*gʰrebʰ- with Grimm's Law *gʰ > g and *bʰ > b, so the Germanic shape grab- is phonologically the expected one. One caveat worth recording: Watkins hedges these forms as 'parallel (imitative) Germanic creations with base *grab-, *grap-', i.e. he suspects expressive re-formation rather than straight inheritance, precisely because the -pp-/-bb- geminates of the West Germanic forms are of an expressive type; the assignment to ghrebh-1 is nevertheless unanimous in the handbooks. Also note that the Germanic noun *graba- 'grave' (OHG grab, English grave) belongs NOT here but to Pokorny's 2. ghrebh- 'to dig, bury' — a distinction Nourai keeps.",
