          "derivation": "Proto-Germanic *grimmaz 'fierce, savage' (< *gʰrem-nó-, with the assimilation *-mn- > *-mm- typical of Germanic) yields OE grim(m), OS/OFris./OHG grimm, ON grimmr 'grim, fierce, hostile'; the o-grade/zero-grade *gram- gives ON gramr 'enraged', OE/OS/OHG gram, MHG gram 'angry', with the causative *gramjan (Goth. gramjan 'to enrage', OHG gremmen); and the zero-grade *grum- gives OHG umbi-grummōn 'to gnaw at', MHG/MLG grummen, MDu grommen 'to mutter angrily, growl', NHG grummen/grummeln 'to grumble, mutter, rumble dully'. Both of Nourai's forms are therefore exactly the two Germanic bases the handbooks set up under this root, and both are cited under it by the reference he gives: AHD/Watkins 'ghrem-. Angry. 1. Germanic *grimmaz ... 2. Germanic *grum- ...'. Pokorny lists the whole set on pp. 458-459.",
