          "derivation": "GRIM is the direct native reflex: Old English grim(m) 'fierce, cruel, severe, painful' < Proto-Germanic *grimma- < *gʰrem-nó-, unbroken into Middle and Modern English (Etymonline: 'grim, Old English grimm ... Proto-Germanic *grimma- ... PIE *ghremno- \"angry\", perhaps imitative of the sound of thunder'); Skeat s.v. Grim: 'A.S. grim; allied to gram, fierce, angry, furious. + Icel. grimmr, gramr; Dan. grim, gram; G. grimm, gram. Allied to Gk. khremizo, khromos, noise. (√GHRAM, from √GHAR.)'. GRUMBLE reaches English by a detour but from the same base: Middle French grommeler, itself from Middle Dutch grommelen, frequentative of grommen 'to grumble, mutter angrily', beside native Middle English grummen; the -b- is intrusive, as in ramble (Klein). So Nourai's Germanic→English arrow holds for both words, with the qualification that grumble is a 16th-century borrowing of a Germanic word through French rather than an inherited Old English form.",
